WEBVTT
Kind: captions

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:05.120 position:63%
We're on a sound stage in Paris.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:06.880 position:63%
It's TV magic. I know.

00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:09.580 position:63%
Netflix says it is
backing out of its effort

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:10.940 position:63%
to acquire Warner Brothers.

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:13.180 position:63%
There's a lot of fear around AI.

00:00:13.180 --> 00:00:15.867 position:63%
A lot of concerns around engagement.

00:00:15.867 --> 00:00:16.753 position:63%
There's plenty of room to

00:00:16.753 --> 00:00:17.586 position:63%
continue on the path that we've been on.

00:00:17.586 --> 00:00:18.419 position:63%
I'm in Korea. This is the
third continent we've been

00:00:28.340 --> 00:00:30.740 position:63%
to in the course of filming this episode,

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:33.860 position:63%
and I'm about to go interview
Ted Sarandos one last time.

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:38.180 position:63%
At first, this seemed like a
pretty straightforward story

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:40.420 position:63%
about a disruptor turned incumbent

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:42.340 position:63%
that changed the way we all watch movies

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:44.360 position:63%
and TV, helped build this global audience

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:46.360 position:63%
for stories around the world.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:49.400 position:63%
But in the last few
weeks, the story changed.

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:51.300 position:63%
This stock has really plunged.

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:52.920 position:63%
Laying off a thousand employees,

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:54.720 position:63%
about 6% of its workforce.

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:56.540 position:63%
The chief content officer's gone,

00:00:56.540 --> 00:00:57.940 position:63%
and the head of the film studio is

00:00:57.940 --> 00:00:58.940 position:63%
gone.

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:00.380 position:63%
They've simply stopped innovating.

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:04.279 position:63%
The entertainment business
is notoriously unpredictable.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:08.980 position:63%
From the first talking
pictures, to television,

00:01:08.980 --> 00:01:12.380 position:63%
to video stores, to the rise
of streaming, you either adapt

00:01:12.380 --> 00:01:14.720 position:63%
to the times or end up

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:16.840 position:63%
with one last location in Bend, Oregon.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:22.840 position:63%
So how does Netflix avoid that fate?

00:01:22.840 --> 00:01:25.380 position:63%
For one thing, it's expanding
its global ambitions much

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:27.260 position:63%
further, investing billions in the

00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:28.460 position:63%
fast-growing Asian market.

00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:31.640 position:63%
It's also doubling down on its tech roots,

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:34.600 position:63%
expanding into new formats,
and of course... AI.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:37.060 position:63%
AI. AI. AI.
AI. AI.

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:40.600 position:63%
The sequence of numbers,
predicting the next token.

00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:42.320 position:63%
Yes, that's Ben Affleck

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:44.980 position:63%
who also works at Netflix since
the streamer bought his AI

00:01:44.980 --> 00:01:46.540 position:63%
company, Interpositive.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:49.880 position:63%
And who isn't shy about
calling for important changes?

00:01:49.880 --> 00:01:51.940 position:63%
I want to know why my
algorithm keeps giving me Matt

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:53.380 position:63%
Damon's picture on The Rip.

00:01:53.380 --> 00:01:54.700 position:63%
instead of you.
Yeah. I mean,

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:56.640 position:63%
Clearly if there's one -
We essentially put you in the

00:01:56.640 --> 00:01:58.900 position:63%
test cell, then not see your own face.

00:02:00.300 --> 00:02:02.420 position:63%
How these bets turn out
will obviously be important

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:04.240 position:63%
for the future of Netflix,

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:07.120 position:63%
but this story is much
bigger than one company.

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:10.040 position:63%
It's about an industry trying
to redefine itself in the face

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:12.100 position:63%
of a technological tidal wave

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:14.140 position:63%
and what it means for the human creativity

00:02:14.140 --> 00:02:15.600 position:63%
and storytelling that we love.

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:19.360 position:63%
So now I have to go ask
Ted some hard questions.

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:25.780 position:63%
But we're getting ahead of ourselves.

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:27.160 position:63%
Let's start at the beginning,

00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:28.920 position:63%
when I first interviewed Sarandos at

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:30.300 position:63%
Netflix headquarters in LA.

00:02:34.740 --> 00:02:36.820 position:63%
So do the stars come through
here or is there a secret door?

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:39.160 position:63%
This is a pretty star-studded
lobby some days. Yeah.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:41.240 position:63%
How often do you get pitched

00:02:41.240 --> 00:02:43.020 position:63%
before you even get to the office?

00:02:43.020 --> 00:02:44.480 position:63%
Well, sometimes at the coffee shop

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:45.480 position:63%
before I get to the office, so...

00:02:49.740 --> 00:02:51.220 position:63%
Ted Sarandos has been at Netflix

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:53.820 position:63%
for almost the entire life of the company.

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:55.580 position:63%
He's overseen many of the bold bets

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:58.220 position:63%
that have made the company what
it is today, from the birth

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:03.220 position:63%
of streaming to the global
push. So it's Emmy's night.

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:06.500 position:63%
Yeah. Do you get a little
nervous on Emmy's night?

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:09.020 position:63%
I'm a competitive soul, so
I'm... And I, and I'm really...

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:11.400 position:63%
And I know how hard
everybody works, so I really,

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:12.660 position:63%
I want them all to win so much.

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:14.420 position:63%
Did you make sure everyone
thanks you in their speech?

00:03:15.740 --> 00:03:18.240 position:63%
That's organic.
Thanks to Ted Sarandos.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:21.120 position:63%
Ted Sarandos.
Ted Sarandos. My friend, Ted.

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:25.100 position:63%
After 25 years, can you
tell the difference between

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:26.580 position:63%
what you love and what you think

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:28.300 position:63%
300 million people will love?

00:03:28.300 --> 00:03:31.620 position:63%
For me, it's always been
this, the, the discipline

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:34.260 position:63%
because I know that my
taste is a little quirky

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:36.800 position:63%
and the things that my, my,
my favorite Netflix show

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:39.120 position:63%
of all time is a show called Lady Dynamite

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:40.980 position:63%
that I think just me and like 10 other

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:42.060 position:63%
people really love too.

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:43.360 position:63%
Welcome to the big leagues, baby girl.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:46.079 position:63%
So what do you mean by

00:03:46.079 --> 00:03:47.079 position:63%
discipline?

00:03:47.079 --> 00:03:48.960 position:63%
Discipline to say this is
I think this will be great.

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:51.219 position:63%
It's not for me. And then the other one,

00:03:51.219 --> 00:03:53.160 position:63%
which is the opposite,
which is I love this,

00:03:53.160 --> 00:03:54.320 position:63%
but no one's going to watch it but me.

00:03:56.060 --> 00:03:58.820 position:63%
Sarandos became Co-CEO in 2020.

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:00.280 position:63%
He's now the face of the company

00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:02.880 position:63%
and an omnipresent figure in Hollywood.

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:04.459 position:63%
But he comes from modest roots,

00:04:04.459 --> 00:04:06.360 position:63%
growing up in the American Southwest

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:08.300 position:63%
where he became obsessed with the movies

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:10.060 position:63%
and worked at an old school video store.

00:04:12.340 --> 00:04:14.020 position:63%
Take me back to growing up in Phoenix

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:16.440 position:63%
and Arizona Video Cassettes West.

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:18.020 position:63%
That's a great title.
It's a great name, right?

00:04:18.020 --> 00:04:19.860 position:63%
It rolls off the tongue. It's very catchy.

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:20.960 position:63%
What was that place like

00:04:20.960 --> 00:04:22.580 position:63%
and how did you get from there to here?

00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:26.140 position:63%
My parents, like my dad
was an electrician, my mom,

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:28.480 position:63%
stay home mom and five kids.

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:31.000 position:63%
And you know, we were
kind of struggling to get

00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:35.440 position:63%
by all the time, but my
mom buys a VCR which

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:37.740 position:63%
seemed like a really frivolous
use of money at the time.

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:39.960 position:63%
And the second video
store in the state opened

00:04:39.960 --> 00:04:41.600 position:63%
around the corner from our house, run

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:43.160 position:63%
by a guy named Dale Mason.

00:04:43.160 --> 00:04:45.200 position:63%
And there was not that much
to rent back in those days.

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:47.520 position:63%
It was just a few Hollywood movies, a lot

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:50.320 position:63%
of weird independent
film and documentaries

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:52.040 position:63%
and foreign language films.

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:54.220 position:63%
And I would go in and just
talk to Dale about movies

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:56.800 position:63%
because I was a movie nerd
and he was a movie nerd

00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:58.920 position:63%
and eventually he just offered me a job.

00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:02.220 position:63%
By the time I left there, I
was running a chain of stores.

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:05.580 position:63%
So for me, working in those
stores was some combination

00:05:05.580 --> 00:05:07.160 position:63%
of business school and film school

00:05:08.260 --> 00:05:09.640 position:63%
that really affected
everything I do today.

00:05:10.520 --> 00:05:12.640 position:63%
So you were chief content
officer for 20 years,

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:14.460 position:63%
you became Co-CEO.

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:17.500 position:63%
You made bets that
looked crazy at the time.

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:21.600 position:63%
How do you know when to take
a big counterintuitive risk?

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:24.700 position:63%
So I think I'd go back
to that 1999 meeting

00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:27.560 position:63%
with Reed when we were talking
about this kind of the value

00:05:27.560 --> 00:05:30.480 position:63%
of a contrarian bet, which his was

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:31.800 position:63%
all entertainment is going

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:33.020 position:63%
to come into the home on the internet.

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:36.560 position:63%
Now that seems pretty intuitive.
In 1999, it was ridiculous.

00:05:36.560 --> 00:05:39.080 position:63%
No entertainment came into
the home on the internet.

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:40.580 position:63%
Like that's high risk,

00:05:40.580 --> 00:05:42.460 position:63%
but we had a big prize if
you could figure it out.

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:47.060 position:63%
That particular risk
turned out about as well

00:05:47.060 --> 00:05:48.860 position:63%
as anything in the
history of entertainment.

00:05:50.680 --> 00:05:52.920 position:63%
Viewers flocked to
streaming, forcing the rest

00:05:52.920 --> 00:05:55.700 position:63%
of the industry to
dance to Netflix's tune.

00:05:55.700 --> 00:05:58.140 position:63%
The company traded like a
tech stock for many years,

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:00.420 position:63%
earning a place in the acronym FAANG.

00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:02.640 position:63%
Yeah, Facebook, Amazon,
Apple, Netflix, and Google.

00:06:03.500 --> 00:06:05.380 position:63%
But lately, FAANG has been supplanted

00:06:05.380 --> 00:06:07.540 position:63%
by the so-called Magnificent Seven,

00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:10.300 position:63%
and there's an N in there,
but it's not Netflix.

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:13.280 position:63%
Subscriber engagement

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:15.880 position:63%
and revenue growth have
slowed in recent years.

00:06:15.880 --> 00:06:17.960 position:63%
And when I interviewed
Sarandos in September,

00:06:17.960 --> 00:06:20.880 position:63%
the stock had fallen 40% from its peak.

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:22.560 position:63%
Still, he seemed pretty optimistic

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:23.640 position:63%
about how things were going.

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:26.280 position:63%
Last year we grew 16%.

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:28.080 position:63%
It's pretty nice growth, I think,

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:31.880 position:63%
and certainly very profitable growth.

00:06:31.880 --> 00:06:34.040 position:63%
I think both of those
things can be solid if

00:06:34.040 --> 00:06:35.560 position:63%
you're really good at what you're doing.

00:06:35.560 --> 00:06:37.620 position:63%
And that means you have
teams around the world,

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:41.080 position:63%
you have teams here in
LA who are picking shows

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:43.820 position:63%
and delivering those shows,
work with creators to make sure

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:45.920 position:63%
that we have the show that
you can't live without,

00:06:45.920 --> 00:06:47.220 position:63%
the movie you can't live without.

00:06:47.220 --> 00:06:48.940 position:63%
We've got to continue to
win that all the time.

00:06:48.940 --> 00:06:50.960 position:63%
And if we do that, then
there's plenty of room

00:06:50.960 --> 00:06:52.380 position:63%
to continue on the path
that we've been on.

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:55.820 position:63%
The question now is, what comes next?

00:06:57.960 --> 00:06:59.960 position:63%
For the past decade, the
company has been betting on the

00:06:59.960 --> 00:07:01.280 position:63%
world outside the US.

00:07:03.680 --> 00:07:05.980 position:63%
During Reed Hastings' keynote
speech at the Consumer

00:07:05.980 --> 00:07:07.880 position:63%
Electronics Show in 2016,

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:12.200 position:63%
Netflix expanded its service
from 30 to 190 countries.

00:07:12.200 --> 00:07:15.320 position:63%
You are witnessing the birth

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:18.320 position:63%
of a global TV network.

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:21.080 position:63%
Since then, it's spent
billions on global productions,

00:07:21.080 --> 00:07:23.420 position:63%
striving to compete with local TV channels

00:07:23.420 --> 00:07:25.540 position:63%
and spawning the
occasional global mega hit.

00:07:27.380 --> 00:07:29.660 position:63%
One such mega hit is Emily in Paris.

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:33.140 position:63%
No, not that Emily in Paris.

00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:37.579 position:63%
I like Paris, but I'm
not really sure Paris likes

00:07:37.579 --> 00:07:38.940 position:63%
me, and maybe that's okay.

00:07:40.239 --> 00:07:42.779 position:63%
Led by Sex in the City
creator, Darren Starr,

00:07:42.779 --> 00:07:45.780 position:63%
Emily in Paris is a hybrid
production mixing American

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:48.200 position:63%
and French actors, producers and crew

00:07:48.200 --> 00:07:49.599 position:63%
and filmed in the city of light.

00:07:51.720 --> 00:07:54.640 position:63%
So we're about to go on set.
They're rolling right now.

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:56.800 position:63%
Red light on, but we're
going to go in in a sec.

00:07:59.820 --> 00:08:03.980 position:63%
It's just so fabulous,
fabulous to see it in person.

00:08:03.980 --> 00:08:05.780 position:63%
It looks so realistic.
So when you're inside,

00:08:05.780 --> 00:08:08.760 position:63%
you actually do feel
like you're in the space.

00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:10.320 position:63%
I know. It's TV magic.
I know.

00:08:10.320 --> 00:08:12.180 position:63%
I actually touched it and
I realized it was fabric

00:08:12.180 --> 00:08:17.139 position:63%
and I was like, "Oh, okay." Hi, Emily.

00:08:18.059 --> 00:08:20.180 position:63%
Not so many. It's so nice to meet you.

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:21.460 position:63%
Thank you for having us.

00:08:21.460 --> 00:08:24.120 position:63%
Thanks for being in Paris.
What are you filming today?

00:08:24.120 --> 00:08:26.220 position:63%
It's actually the
final scene we're filming

00:08:27.160 --> 00:08:30.560 position:63%
in the series at Emily
and Mindy's apartment.

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:33.280 position:63%
Their final scene together.
It's bittersweet for sure.

00:08:34.359 --> 00:08:36.319 position:63%
I know you were just
shooting your last scene

00:08:36.319 --> 00:08:37.680 position:63%
with Mindy in your apartment.

00:08:37.680 --> 00:08:39.760 position:63%
I know, which I didn't realize
until they yelled, "Cut,"

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:42.099 position:63%
and then we were like, "Wait,
what?" And it makes sense

00:08:42.099 --> 00:08:43.340 position:63%
because we are coming to an end.

00:08:43.340 --> 00:08:45.200 position:63%
Is it emotional?
Yes.

00:08:45.200 --> 00:08:46.680 position:63%
Anytime I work with Ashley,

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:48.280 position:63%
we can make a funny scene emotional just

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:49.760 position:63%
because we're so connected.

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:53.140 position:63%
Did you have any idea
it would be this big?

00:08:53.140 --> 00:08:55.080 position:63%
No. You can never
expect any show you do to

00:08:56.600 --> 00:08:58.060 position:63%
be a success on that level.

00:08:58.060 --> 00:08:59.620 position:63%
So many things have to go right

00:08:59.620 --> 00:09:01.840 position:63%
that you just never know when
it's going to come together

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:04.460 position:63%
because there's something about that.

00:09:04.460 --> 00:09:06.800 position:63%
It's a little bit magical when it happens.

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:10.420 position:63%
You've created hits
across every modern TV era.

00:09:10.420 --> 00:09:12.740 position:63%
What have you gained and
what have you lost by sort

00:09:12.740 --> 00:09:16.560 position:63%
of the way how we watch has evolved?

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:20.160 position:63%
The binging aspect of
dropping everything at once

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:23.180 position:63%
is something that was a big
sort of sea change for me

00:09:24.860 --> 00:09:28.040 position:63%
because I feel like I've
always written shows

00:09:28.040 --> 00:09:32.520 position:63%
that are always about having
cliffhangers built into the

00:09:32.520 --> 00:09:34.720 position:63%
ending to pull people into that next week

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:36.860 position:63%
to make them come back for next week.

00:09:36.860 --> 00:09:41.340 position:63%
But to have a platform where
you see everything at once,

00:09:41.340 --> 00:09:43.000 position:63%
it was like a bomb going off.

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:45.040 position:63%
Now I'm hooked.

00:09:49.720 --> 00:09:53.520 position:63%
Okay, let me try one more
time. You ready? Yep. Okay.

00:09:56.360 --> 00:09:58.220 position:63%
It's so hard to get a clean shot here.

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:00.880 position:63%
It's quite the tourist destination.

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:05.240 position:63%
Okay, so we've been waiting
here for over an hour, trying

00:10:05.240 --> 00:10:09.320 position:63%
to get a clear shot of the
door to Emily's apartment,

00:10:09.320 --> 00:10:11.840 position:63%
and there have been so
many tourists walking

00:10:11.840 --> 00:10:13.320 position:63%
by to take pictures.

00:10:13.320 --> 00:10:15.980 position:63%
It's just been impossible.
These people are so excited.

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:18.480 position:63%
Let's just interview them. Ask if they

00:10:18.480 --> 00:10:20.060 position:63%
want to be interviewed.

00:10:20.060 --> 00:10:21.940 position:63%
Are you here just to
see Emily's apartment?

00:10:21.940 --> 00:10:24.560 position:63%
Just for this location, yes.
And then restaurant there.

00:10:24.560 --> 00:10:27.040 position:63%
I feel like what Emily felt
like while she was here.

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:31.833 position:63%
It's the first time I've been in Paris.

00:10:31.833 --> 00:10:32.666 position:63%
Is it everything you
dreamed it would be?

00:10:34.760 --> 00:10:36.740 position:63%
Yeah. In the movies, it seemed bigger.

00:10:36.740 --> 00:10:38.080 position:63%
So what do you think of Emily in Paris?

00:10:39.040 --> 00:10:41.920 position:63%
I don't like this shoe
because it's very cliche.

00:10:53.760 --> 00:10:56.720 position:63%
Love it or hate it, Emily in
Paris has inspired a massive

00:10:56.720 --> 00:10:59.660 position:63%
wave of tourism, as many as 4% of visitors

00:10:59.660 --> 00:11:01.300 position:63%
to Paris, according to one study.

00:11:02.200 --> 00:11:05.520 position:63%
This is what Ted Sarandos likes
to call the Netflix effect,

00:11:05.520 --> 00:11:08.380 position:63%
the idea that what we watch
can shape not just culture,

00:11:08.380 --> 00:11:09.980 position:63%
but where we go and what we buy.

00:11:11.000 --> 00:11:14.720 position:63%
Netflix claims it's pumped
$325 billion into the global

00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:18.400 position:63%
economy, one chess set,
Salvador Dali mask,

00:11:18.400 --> 00:11:20.060 position:63%
or fashionable beret at a time.

00:11:21.560 --> 00:11:25.120 position:63%
Because Emily wears a beret now.

00:11:25.120 --> 00:11:29.220 position:63%
Every people want to discover
the fashion style of Emily.

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:31.080 position:63%
It's very ritual.

00:11:31.080 --> 00:11:31.913 position:63%
I'm into that.

00:11:36.460 --> 00:11:39.960 position:63%
Not everyone sees Netflix's
global reach as a net good.

00:11:39.960 --> 00:11:42.900 position:63%
When the streamer first
launched in France in 2014,

00:11:42.900 --> 00:11:44.780 position:63%
it was seen as an unwelcome disruptor

00:11:44.780 --> 00:11:47.100 position:63%
by the country's
protectionist film industry,

00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:48.440 position:63%
earning the nickname Tsunami.

00:11:49.620 --> 00:11:52.320 position:63%
It's a tough market for
anybody outside of France.

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:55.180 position:63%
There are things that seem
quite foreign to an American,

00:11:55.180 --> 00:12:00.020 position:63%
like the idea of chronology,
meaning it's a matter of law

00:12:00.020 --> 00:12:02.620 position:63%
when movies come to television.

00:12:02.620 --> 00:12:05.260 position:63%
But they believe in France that all this

00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:08.540 position:63%
cultural regulation has
yielded them a better product.

00:12:08.540 --> 00:12:10.960 position:63%
You talk about the Netflix
effect as a force for good,

00:12:10.960 --> 00:12:14.220 position:63%
but we've heard the
flip side, over tourism,

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:16.100 position:63%
questions about authenticity,

00:12:16.100 --> 00:12:18.980 position:63%
pressure on local creators
taking more than you give.

00:12:18.980 --> 00:12:21.420 position:63%
How do you think about
the potential downsides?

00:12:21.420 --> 00:12:23.660 position:63%
Well, look, I would say
on the over-tourism part,

00:12:23.660 --> 00:12:24.820 position:63%
I don't know how you solve for that.

00:12:24.820 --> 00:12:26.940 position:63%
It makes something so great
that people want to see it.

00:12:26.940 --> 00:12:28.900 position:63%
That's exciting. And it's how you manage

00:12:28.900 --> 00:12:30.560 position:63%
that locally is really important.

00:12:30.560 --> 00:12:33.680 position:63%
And the part about do we
take more than we give

00:12:33.680 --> 00:12:35.660 position:63%
and those things, that's clearly not true.

00:12:35.660 --> 00:12:37.900 position:63%
We never came into this market trying

00:12:37.900 --> 00:12:39.720 position:63%
to get something for nothing.

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:41.440 position:63%
We always had to clear the
market, meaning we always had

00:12:41.440 --> 00:12:42.820 position:63%
to be the highest bidder

00:12:42.820 --> 00:12:45.540 position:63%
for every new space we've
gotten into in content,

00:12:45.540 --> 00:12:48.120 position:63%
and then we've got to be
able to then make it work.

00:12:48.120 --> 00:12:49.820 position:63%
So we've taken on that risk.

00:12:49.820 --> 00:12:51.280 position:63%
This past week, my wife

00:12:51.280 --> 00:12:53.300 position:63%
and I were both awarded the Legion

00:12:53.300 --> 00:12:55.000 position:63%
of Honor from President Macron.

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:57.640 position:63%
But when we did that, he reminded me that

00:12:57.640 --> 00:12:59.200 position:63%
nine years ago, Netflix was the devil.

00:13:00.900 --> 00:13:01.940 position:63%
You've come a long way.
Yes.

00:13:03.780 --> 00:13:06.420 position:63%
Beyond American co-productions
like Emily in Paris,

00:13:06.420 --> 00:13:09.460 position:63%
Netflix is also betting big
on fully local productions

00:13:09.460 --> 00:13:12.300 position:63%
driven by local talent in
hopes of creating content

00:13:12.300 --> 00:13:14.320 position:63%
with authentic un-Hollywood appeal.

00:13:15.380 --> 00:13:17.840 position:63%
Squid Game is, of course,
the most well-known example.

00:13:18.720 --> 00:13:20.580 position:63%
Since that 2021 smash hit,

00:13:20.580 --> 00:13:23.340 position:63%
Netflix's Korean operation
has become a juggernaut in its

00:13:23.340 --> 00:13:25.620 position:63%
own right, investing billions of dollars

00:13:25.620 --> 00:13:28.780 position:63%
and supercharging the
popularity of K content,

00:13:28.780 --> 00:13:30.580 position:63%
but creating tensions with the local film

00:13:30.580 --> 00:13:31.800 position:63%
industry in the process.

00:13:36.700 --> 00:13:38.400 position:63%
Do you think I can walk
with the Squid game guy?

00:13:41.400 --> 00:13:43.920 position:63%
When we arrived in Seoul,
Netflix was holding a festival

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:45.840 position:63%
to celebrate 10 years in Korea

00:13:45.840 --> 00:13:48.940 position:63%
and fete its homegrown talent
like fashion model turned

00:13:48.940 --> 00:13:50.140 position:63%
actor Byeon Woo-seok.

00:13:51.340 --> 00:13:53.280 position:63%
You might want to say hi
to your fans out there.

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:06.120 position:63%
What are some things

00:14:06.120 --> 00:14:08.160 position:63%
that Korea still does better
than every other region?

00:14:09.100 --> 00:14:11.000 position:63%
I honestly think it is
that kind of attention

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:13.600 position:63%
to production detail, production quality.

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:15.440 position:63%
80% of our members have watched

00:14:15.440 --> 00:14:17.120 position:63%
Korean content around the world.

00:14:17.120 --> 00:14:20.460 position:63%
So there's something really
about Korean storytelling

00:14:20.460 --> 00:14:22.140 position:63%
that resonates with the world.

00:14:22.140 --> 00:14:26.220 position:63%
And I think that cross pollination
between food and fashion

00:14:26.220 --> 00:14:29.100 position:63%
and music, all those things come together.

00:14:29.100 --> 00:14:30.900 position:63%
Is this just a show? Is this a movie?

00:14:30.900 --> 00:14:32.780 position:63%
Or is it the beginning of something else?

00:14:41.780 --> 00:14:44.120 position:63%
Netflix's involvement in
Korea has been spearheaded from

00:14:44.120 --> 00:14:45.860 position:63%
the start by Minyoung Kim,

00:14:45.860 --> 00:14:47.400 position:63%
Vice President of Content for Asia.

00:14:48.280 --> 00:14:50.840 position:63%
Korean entertainment companies
had mostly given Netflix the

00:14:50.840 --> 00:14:53.600 position:63%
cold shoulder until Kim
applied her insider's touch.

00:14:54.520 --> 00:14:57.540 position:63%
What was the approach
originally and why was it wrong?

00:14:57.540 --> 00:15:00.780 position:63%
The way that we used to do
business, it was a little more

00:15:00.780 --> 00:15:04.640 position:63%
of a Hollywood style
and very transactional.

00:15:04.640 --> 00:15:08.040 position:63%
In Korea, it's really important
to build that relationship

00:15:08.040 --> 00:15:11.960 position:63%
before having that
transactional conversation.

00:15:11.960 --> 00:15:14.680 position:63%
I would have my backpack
with my laptop in it,

00:15:14.680 --> 00:15:16.580 position:63%
and then I will go door to door

00:15:16.580 --> 00:15:19.380 position:63%
to these production
companies, meeting producers,

00:15:19.380 --> 00:15:22.180 position:63%
meeting creators, explaining to them

00:15:22.180 --> 00:15:23.819 position:63%
what Netflix wants to do.

00:15:23.819 --> 00:15:27.879 position:63%
There are a lot of nights where
I drink with the partners,

00:15:27.879 --> 00:15:32.480 position:63%
build bombshell, whatever that is.

00:15:32.480 --> 00:15:35.460 position:63%
And I think the fact that as a Korean,

00:15:36.620 --> 00:15:40.100 position:63%
telling the Korean partners how we

00:15:40.100 --> 00:15:42.579 position:63%
as Netflix value Korean content, I think

00:15:42.579 --> 00:15:45.740 position:63%
that coming from one
of them was important.

00:15:45.740 --> 00:15:48.360 position:63%
How is storytelling different
in different countries?

00:15:48.360 --> 00:15:51.020 position:63%
How does what audiences want differ?

00:15:51.020 --> 00:15:53.799 position:63%
Let's look at a mystery,
whether it's a murder mystery.

00:15:54.780 --> 00:15:58.420 position:63%
So in a Western mystery, traditionally,

00:15:58.420 --> 00:16:00.220 position:63%
the emphasis is on who.

00:16:01.439 --> 00:16:04.620 position:63%
In a Japanese mystery, a lot of the times,

00:16:04.620 --> 00:16:06.279 position:63%
often the emphasis is on how.

00:16:07.539 --> 00:16:12.539 position:63%
In Korean crime mystery, a lot
of the times it's about why.

00:16:12.959 --> 00:16:16.080 position:63%
Korean romance tends to be happy ending,

00:16:17.200 --> 00:16:22.160 position:63%
whereas a lot of the times
with my Chinese language team,

00:16:22.160 --> 00:16:23.300 position:63%
a lot of the romances

00:16:23.300 --> 00:16:26.080 position:63%
or human story, it's
always about sad ending.

00:16:26.080 --> 00:16:28.320 position:63%
There's a lot of tears that gets involved.

00:16:28.320 --> 00:16:33.140 position:63%
So every culture has a very
different approach even

00:16:33.140 --> 00:16:35.140 position:63%
to the same types of storytelling.

00:16:37.940 --> 00:16:41.120 position:63%
Here in Korea, the Netflix
effect is in full swing.

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:44.000 position:63%
One example, this restaurant
owned by Nara Yun,

00:16:44.000 --> 00:16:47.140 position:63%
a contestant on Netflix's
Culinary Class Wars.

00:16:47.140 --> 00:16:49.619 position:63%
Some have credited the show
with driving increased business

00:16:49.619 --> 00:16:50.980 position:63%
to Soul's fine dining scene.

00:16:52.440 --> 00:16:54.920 position:63%
Netflix has also capitalized
on the global interest in

00:16:54.920 --> 00:16:57.880 position:63%
Korean culture by producing
the smash hit K-pop Demon

00:16:57.880 --> 00:17:00.159 position:63%
Hunters, which was made in North America,

00:17:00.159 --> 00:17:02.180 position:63%
but drew inspiration
from traditional Korean

00:17:02.180 --> 00:17:03.320 position:63%
designs and folklore.

00:17:05.540 --> 00:17:09.820 position:63%
Oh, look. It's the hat.
All right. I'm ready.

00:17:10.920 --> 00:17:15.920 position:63%
Ready for Halloween.
The movie is Netflix's

00:17:15.940 --> 00:17:18.099 position:63%
biggest original feature of all time,

00:17:18.099 --> 00:17:20.340 position:63%
racking up half a billion views.

00:17:20.340 --> 00:17:21.660 position:63%
For the inevitable sequel,

00:17:21.660 --> 00:17:23.980 position:63%
Netflix is planning a
theatrical release, part

00:17:23.980 --> 00:17:25.620 position:63%
of a recent softening of the company's

00:17:25.620 --> 00:17:26.720 position:63%
streaming only strategy.

00:17:28.580 --> 00:17:30.240 position:63%
I was wondering if you
thought it could have been

00:17:30.240 --> 00:17:31.900 position:63%
bigger if it was in

00:17:31.900 --> 00:17:32.900 position:63%
theaters.

00:17:32.900 --> 00:17:34.940 position:63%
No, I'm positive part two will be huge in

00:17:34.940 --> 00:17:36.040 position:63%
theaters and big on Netflix.

00:17:39.560 --> 00:17:41.400 position:63%
But once again, the Netflix effect

00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:42.540 position:63%
hasn't made everyone happy.

00:17:43.640 --> 00:17:46.620 position:63%
Bloomberg's Sohee Kim has been
reporting on Netflix's impact on

00:17:46.620 --> 00:17:48.000 position:63%
the Korean entertainment industry.

00:17:48.840 --> 00:17:52.880 position:63%
All of the money flows
into the Netflix originals

00:17:52.880 --> 00:17:55.260 position:63%
and the kind of like a cause of fear

00:17:55.260 --> 00:17:57.900 position:63%
that Netflix might control too much

00:17:57.900 --> 00:18:00.680 position:63%
of content pipeline in the Korea,

00:18:00.680 --> 00:18:04.620 position:63%
and the dominance has grown
immensely over the past years.

00:18:06.080 --> 00:18:09.040 position:63%
So the money dried up to
the traditional creators

00:18:09.040 --> 00:18:12.140 position:63%
and the studios behind Korean dramas

00:18:12.140 --> 00:18:15.920 position:63%
and films, which is causing
some concerns in the content

00:18:15.920 --> 00:18:18.460 position:63%
industry, whether this
is a sustainable model.

00:18:18.460 --> 00:18:22.500 position:63%
How do producers and actors
feel about Netflix here?

00:18:22.500 --> 00:18:26.220 position:63%
Working with Netflix is
kind of like a proud moment

00:18:26.220 --> 00:18:27.620 position:63%
for creators and actors

00:18:27.620 --> 00:18:31.580 position:63%
because once they reach Netflix's platform

00:18:31.580 --> 00:18:36.020 position:63%
and becomes a global hit,
well, it's a national pride,

00:18:36.020 --> 00:18:38.080 position:63%
but at the same time, some creators

00:18:38.080 --> 00:18:42.140 position:63%
and studios have to give
up valuable IP franchises.

00:18:42.140 --> 00:18:46.320 position:63%
Because Netflix has immense
power over the Korean content

00:18:46.320 --> 00:18:49.360 position:63%
industry, well, they have
the negotiation power.

00:18:50.320 --> 00:18:52.440 position:63%
Case in point, Squid Game,

00:18:52.440 --> 00:18:55.300 position:63%
whose creator didn't initially
share in the enormous upside

00:18:55.300 --> 00:18:56.580 position:63%
from the show's first season.

00:18:58.020 --> 00:18:59.700 position:63%
If a Korean producer
makes the next Squid

00:18:59.700 --> 00:19:00.720 position:63%
Game, who gets rich?

00:19:02.180 --> 00:19:03.013 position:63%
Netflix.

00:19:07.840 --> 00:19:11.200 position:63%
Netflix has obviously helped
Korean content go global,

00:19:11.200 --> 00:19:13.860 position:63%
but critics say it's also driving up costs

00:19:13.860 --> 00:19:15.260 position:63%
and squeezing local players.

00:19:15.260 --> 00:19:17.040 position:63%
How do you see that tension?

00:19:17.040 --> 00:19:22.040 position:63%
We are still a little
fraction of the TV time

00:19:22.160 --> 00:19:24.240 position:63%
that our audiences engage.

00:19:25.400 --> 00:19:27.440 position:63%
Driving the cost wise, I think it's really

00:19:28.280 --> 00:19:30.580 position:63%
what we've enabled is ability

00:19:30.580 --> 00:19:32.340 position:63%
to dream bigger, have bigger vision.

00:19:34.680 --> 00:19:37.980 position:63%
All of our shows and all of
our films that we film here,

00:19:37.980 --> 00:19:39.880 position:63%
they're all made with Korean producers,

00:19:39.880 --> 00:19:41.480 position:63%
Korean writers, Korean cast.

00:19:43.000 --> 00:19:44.940 position:63%
We're not toe dipping into the country.

00:19:44.940 --> 00:19:46.680 position:63%
We're a part of the
creative ecosystem here.

00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:54.180 position:63%
You talked about how Asia
could be the next Hollywood.

00:19:54.180 --> 00:19:56.780 position:63%
What's still missing to make that happen?

00:19:56.780 --> 00:19:59.080 position:63%
How can Netflix help build that?

00:19:59.080 --> 00:20:03.300 position:63%
Well, I actually don't aspire
to be the next Hollywood.

00:20:03.300 --> 00:20:07.520 position:63%
I hope that we can be our
version of what we can be.

00:20:07.520 --> 00:20:12.020 position:63%
What's great is that every
country has its strength.

00:20:12.020 --> 00:20:16.800 position:63%
Korea has a great pool of
creators and creative community.

00:20:16.800 --> 00:20:20.120 position:63%
Japan, I always think is
an ocean of imagination.

00:20:20.120 --> 00:20:24.740 position:63%
Thailand, the production
ecosystem is really robust.

00:20:24.740 --> 00:20:29.460 position:63%
My hope is that Netflix can play the role

00:20:29.460 --> 00:20:32.760 position:63%
of connecting them
together for us to become

00:20:33.780 --> 00:20:38.620 position:63%
a sizable industry altogether
so that we are able to build

00:20:38.620 --> 00:20:40.740 position:63%
that sustainability ourselves as well.

00:20:46.380 --> 00:20:47.460 position:63%
The rise of global film

00:20:47.460 --> 00:20:49.120 position:63%
and TV production has been accompanied

00:20:49.120 --> 00:20:51.600 position:63%
by a less bullish story in Hollywood.

00:20:51.600 --> 00:20:54.760 position:63%
Los Angeles film and TV
production is down 46%

00:20:54.760 --> 00:20:56.020 position:63%
from pre-pandemic levels.

00:20:56.860 --> 00:20:59.500 position:63%
Lawmakers have introduced a
generous tax credit endorsed

00:20:59.500 --> 00:21:02.420 position:63%
by President Trump to
encourage more US production,

00:21:02.420 --> 00:21:04.880 position:63%
but it remains to be seen whether
the trend can be reversed.

00:21:08.100 --> 00:21:09.760 position:63%
Production has gone truly global.

00:21:09.760 --> 00:21:11.100 position:63%
You're also building out this big

00:21:11.100 --> 00:21:13.100 position:63%
production hub in New Jersey.

00:21:13.100 --> 00:21:17.080 position:63%
Where are you seeing the center
of gravity shift beyond LA?

00:21:17.080 --> 00:21:18.340 position:63%
Yeah, look, and I would say that the

00:21:18.340 --> 00:21:19.660 position:63%
reason why there is some center

00:21:19.660 --> 00:21:21.860 position:63%
of gravity shift in
production from LA is this

00:21:21.860 --> 00:21:24.200 position:63%
California just has not
been very competitive

00:21:24.200 --> 00:21:25.720 position:63%
for the production incentives

00:21:25.720 --> 00:21:29.060 position:63%
that folks are trying all over
the world to pull production

00:21:29.060 --> 00:21:31.840 position:63%
to their cities and states and countries.

00:21:31.840 --> 00:21:34.560 position:63%
And because it's just a dynamic
thing that people are going

00:21:34.560 --> 00:21:38.440 position:63%
to say yes to these
incentives, that you see a lot

00:21:38.440 --> 00:21:41.040 position:63%
of production move out of
California, which is a shame

00:21:41.040 --> 00:21:43.540 position:63%
because the talent base is here,

00:21:43.540 --> 00:21:45.200 position:63%
the creative history is here.

00:21:46.440 --> 00:21:49.940 position:63%
I hate to lose that concentration
of great storytelling,

00:21:49.940 --> 00:21:52.520 position:63%
but at the same time you have
to celebrate the idea that

00:21:52.520 --> 00:21:54.000 position:63%
it's truly global now.

00:21:54.000 --> 00:21:56.320 position:63%
Will production meaningfully
come back to Hollywood

00:21:56.320 --> 00:21:57.660 position:63%
or has that ship sailed?

00:21:57.660 --> 00:21:59.460 position:63%
Yeah, we shoot a lot in Hollywood.

00:21:59.460 --> 00:22:04.460 position:63%
So we shoot about 30 productions
coming up in California,

00:22:05.160 --> 00:22:06.240 position:63%
a lot of it right here in Hollywood.

00:22:07.160 --> 00:22:08.500 position:63%
So I don't think it has sailed.

00:22:08.500 --> 00:22:11.820 position:63%
I think it's going to be a
constant fine tuning of how

00:22:11.820 --> 00:22:14.140 position:63%
to make it more and more
attractive to take advantage

00:22:14.140 --> 00:22:15.240 position:63%
of the talent pool and the

00:22:15.240 --> 00:22:16.320 position:63%
infrastructure that's already here.

00:22:21.440 --> 00:22:22.920 position:63%
So how's office life treating you?

00:22:23.760 --> 00:22:26.240 position:63%
Great. I mean, especially this office.

00:22:26.240 --> 00:22:28.240 position:63%
I wish this was my office.
This is really nice.

00:22:28.240 --> 00:22:29.840 position:63%
Are you making sure he clocks in or...

00:22:29.840 --> 00:22:31.780 position:63%
Yeah, at least 12 hours a day.

00:22:31.780 --> 00:22:34.100 position:63%
I mean, you are a Netflix
employee now, right?

00:22:34.100 --> 00:22:36.920 position:63%
I think I'm a Netflix partner something.

00:22:36.920 --> 00:22:38.400 position:63%
I have some special
status. Senior advisor.

00:22:39.500 --> 00:22:43.060 position:63%
Ben Affleck has worn many hats and cowls

00:22:43.060 --> 00:22:45.340 position:63%
and space suits in his
career in the movies.

00:22:46.340 --> 00:22:49.780 position:63%
These days, he's taken on a new
mission, working at Netflix,

00:22:49.780 --> 00:22:51.960 position:63%
which acquired his AI
company, Interpositive,

00:22:51.960 --> 00:22:53.220 position:63%
for nearly $600 million.

00:22:54.520 --> 00:22:56.500 position:63%
It was a merger.
It's a merger. They call it a merger.

00:22:56.500 --> 00:22:59.180 position:63%
I was like, "How does the
elephant merge with the pebble?

00:22:59.180 --> 00:23:02.860 position:63%
Mostly elephants after it turns out."

00:23:02.860 --> 00:23:05.680 position:63%
Interpositive has an
attractive pitch for Hollywood.

00:23:05.680 --> 00:23:07.080 position:63%
Powerful new AI tools

00:23:07.080 --> 00:23:09.640 position:63%
that work alongside human
creators rather than

00:23:09.640 --> 00:23:11.220 position:63%
replacing them.

00:23:11.220 --> 00:23:14.840 position:63%
This is not making movies
from just typing something.

00:23:14.840 --> 00:23:16.020 position:63%
I don't personally think

00:23:16.020 --> 00:23:19.520 position:63%
that will ever be the way
any good movies are made.

00:23:19.520 --> 00:23:21.160 position:63%
It's been a turbulent decade

00:23:21.160 --> 00:23:23.100 position:63%
for the entertainment business.

00:23:23.100 --> 00:23:24.860 position:63%
Theater attendance is up this year,

00:23:24.860 --> 00:23:28.400 position:63%
but still below pre-pandemic
levels, and the rise of AI

00:23:28.400 --> 00:23:30.960 position:63%
and new platforms is
creating disruptive change.

00:23:32.940 --> 00:23:35.820 position:63%
Can Netflix keep up in
this new media landscape?

00:23:35.820 --> 00:23:38.020 position:63%
And what does this all mean for
the future of entertainment?

00:23:45.400 --> 00:23:47.660 position:63%
Netflix's global expansion
has made the company

00:23:47.660 --> 00:23:49.180 position:63%
ubiquitous all over the world.

00:23:51.820 --> 00:23:54.700 position:63%
But lately, problems are
brewing back in the US

00:23:54.700 --> 00:23:56.100 position:63%
where the streamer has to fight tooth

00:23:56.100 --> 00:23:57.420 position:63%
and nail to grow engagement.

00:23:58.840 --> 00:24:00.800 position:63%
Netflix's share of total viewed hours

00:24:00.800 --> 00:24:02.440 position:63%
of streaming is still growing,

00:24:02.440 --> 00:24:04.600 position:63%
but YouTube has opened up a sizable lead.

00:24:05.820 --> 00:24:08.580 position:63%
And while people spend more
time watching Netflix's original

00:24:08.580 --> 00:24:11.520 position:63%
series than all the other
major streamers combined,

00:24:11.520 --> 00:24:13.500 position:63%
you're only as good as your last hit,

00:24:13.500 --> 00:24:15.360 position:63%
and it's been a minute
since Netflix had a new

00:24:15.360 --> 00:24:16.660 position:63%
culture-defining smash.

00:24:18.020 --> 00:24:20.600 position:63%
To make matters worse,
viewers tend not to stick

00:24:20.600 --> 00:24:22.480 position:63%
with Netflix shows after the first season.

00:24:24.240 --> 00:24:26.060 position:63%
So the company's been
trying some new things

00:24:26.060 --> 00:24:27.400 position:63%
to get back in the zeitgeist.

00:24:28.320 --> 00:24:30.140 position:63%
For example, buying a movie studio.

00:24:31.220 --> 00:24:33.760 position:63%
But for better or worse,
that didn't happen.

00:24:33.760 --> 00:24:35.980 position:63%
Paramount is making a hostile $30

00:24:35.980 --> 00:24:38.980 position:63%
a share cash takeover bid
for all of Warner Brothers.

00:24:38.980 --> 00:24:40.700 position:63%
Netflix is declining

00:24:40.700 --> 00:24:42.600 position:63%
to raise its offer for Warner Brothers.

00:24:42.600 --> 00:24:44.120 position:63%
Paramount, Skydance,

00:24:44.120 --> 00:24:46.280 position:63%
and Warner Brothers closed their merger.

00:24:46.280 --> 00:24:48.600 position:63%
They showed them the
money and they said yes.

00:24:49.520 --> 00:24:51.780 position:63%
We would've liked to have
had the Warner Brothers studio,

00:24:51.780 --> 00:24:53.940 position:63%
obviously, where we bid for it,

00:24:53.940 --> 00:24:57.560 position:63%
but it was in that kind of
nice to have category versus

00:24:57.560 --> 00:25:00.020 position:63%
we'll take it at any price
and fight to the finish.

00:25:00.020 --> 00:25:02.980 position:63%
You were willing to spend
$83 billion on this deal, which

00:25:02.980 --> 00:25:06.640 position:63%
to some looked like you
were trying to buy growth.

00:25:06.640 --> 00:25:10.560 position:63%
Do you need another big deal
to get to where you want to go?

00:25:10.560 --> 00:25:13.160 position:63%
No. We've not really gone
through any of these major

00:25:13.160 --> 00:25:15.240 position:63%
acquisitions to get here,

00:25:15.240 --> 00:25:16.920 position:63%
and the next growth is

00:25:16.920 --> 00:25:19.100 position:63%
what we continue to be pretty organic.

00:25:19.100 --> 00:25:21.600 position:63%
So you're trying a lot of
new things, games, sports,

00:25:21.600 --> 00:25:25.260 position:63%
podcasts, more theaters,
experiences, YouTubers.

00:25:26.480 --> 00:25:28.940 position:63%
Which of those is working
better than you hoped,

00:25:28.940 --> 00:25:32.040 position:63%
and which isn't working
as well as you hoped yet?

00:25:32.040 --> 00:25:33.760 position:63%
Well, they're all pretty new,

00:25:33.760 --> 00:25:35.860 position:63%
and they're all competing

00:25:35.860 --> 00:25:38.400 position:63%
with about 200 billion hours of watching.

00:25:38.400 --> 00:25:41.840 position:63%
So it's a steep hill to climb.

00:25:41.840 --> 00:25:43.100 position:63%
Podcasts are super interesting

00:25:43.100 --> 00:25:45.320 position:63%
because people are watching them when they

00:25:45.320 --> 00:25:47.040 position:63%
normally don't stream Netflix.

00:25:47.040 --> 00:25:49.380 position:63%
Live sports has been phenomenal.

00:25:49.380 --> 00:25:51.620 position:63%
Our live initiative isn't just sports,

00:25:51.620 --> 00:25:53.600 position:63%
but it is this idea of live viewing,

00:25:53.600 --> 00:25:55.620 position:63%
so we'll bet more and more on that too.

00:25:55.620 --> 00:25:58.180 position:63%
Is YouTube the one to beat?
I don't know.

00:25:58.180 --> 00:26:00.780 position:63%
I think it's interesting that
we do very different things.

00:26:00.780 --> 00:26:03.680 position:63%
You have signed creators
to these big deals.

00:26:03.680 --> 00:26:05.900 position:63%
YouTube is now paying
some creators millions

00:26:05.900 --> 00:26:07.440 position:63%
of dollars not to work with you.

00:26:07.440 --> 00:26:09.000 position:63%
Have you struck a nerve there?

00:26:09.000 --> 00:26:14.000 position:63%
I don't know. I would say
this, that we look for creators

00:26:14.000 --> 00:26:16.320 position:63%
and creative talent all over the place.

00:26:16.320 --> 00:26:18.800 position:63%
We're doing an original
show with Netflix.

00:26:18.800 --> 00:26:20.460 position:63%
I mean, basically the top 50

00:26:20.460 --> 00:26:24.540 position:63%
to 100 channels on YouTube
now are starting to look

00:26:24.540 --> 00:26:26.940 position:63%
a lot like production companies.

00:26:26.940 --> 00:26:28.060 position:63%
YouTube, to some people,

00:26:28.060 --> 00:26:30.040 position:63%
is looking a lot more like television,

00:26:30.040 --> 00:26:32.240 position:63%
and some folks say Netflix
is looking more like YouTube

00:26:32.240 --> 00:26:34.600 position:63%
with some of the newer things

00:26:34.600 --> 00:26:36.860 position:63%
that you're carrying. How
do you respond to that?

00:26:36.860 --> 00:26:39.740 position:63%
We draw these borders in
ways that viewers don't.

00:26:39.740 --> 00:26:42.860 position:63%
I think that a podcast
is a modern talk show.

00:26:44.100 --> 00:26:47.400 position:63%
So is that look, does
that feel like YouTube

00:26:47.400 --> 00:26:50.780 position:63%
because it's a podcast instead
of a late night comedy show?

00:26:50.780 --> 00:26:52.460 position:63%
I don't think so. I
think it's just not the

00:26:52.460 --> 00:26:54.040 position:63%
way people look at it anymore.

00:26:54.040 --> 00:26:55.360 position:63%
So I just think it evolved.

00:26:55.360 --> 00:26:56.800 position:63%
Would you rather have the next Stranger

00:26:56.800 --> 00:26:58.100 position:63%
Things or the next Mr.

00:26:58.100 --> 00:26:59.100 position:63%
Beast?

00:27:00.860 --> 00:27:03.140 position:63%
It depends on what
mood I was in, I guess.

00:27:03.140 --> 00:27:04.160 position:63%
By the way, why not both?

00:27:08.800 --> 00:27:10.980 position:63%
If Netflix has an edge
in the streaming game

00:27:10.980 --> 00:27:13.520 position:63%
beyond its deep pockets,
it might be its tech.

00:27:14.700 --> 00:27:17.640 position:63%
Netflix's famous recommendation
algorithm has been quietly

00:27:17.640 --> 00:27:19.760 position:63%
training on the tastes of
viewers around the world

00:27:19.760 --> 00:27:22.380 position:63%
for years, helping to shape
the company's strategy.

00:27:23.620 --> 00:27:25.780 position:63%
Data has always been a
huge advantage for Netflix,

00:27:25.780 --> 00:27:29.500 position:63%
and you now have more data
than ever about how we watch.

00:27:29.500 --> 00:27:30.780 position:63%
How do you actually use that data?

00:27:31.820 --> 00:27:35.380 position:63%
Mostly it's designed to
help you find something.

00:27:35.380 --> 00:27:38.600 position:63%
The other business use would
be things like historical data

00:27:38.600 --> 00:27:40.440 position:63%
on how other things are performed.

00:27:40.440 --> 00:27:42.580 position:63%
So it's not really a great indicator

00:27:42.580 --> 00:27:44.340 position:63%
of should you make this show or not,

00:27:44.340 --> 00:27:46.540 position:63%
but it is one that says if this show works

00:27:46.540 --> 00:27:48.940 position:63%
and it's great, it could be this big.

00:27:48.940 --> 00:27:51.020 position:63%
So what would you never use it for?

00:27:51.020 --> 00:27:54.080 position:63%
To reverse engineer a
story, a storytelling,

00:27:54.940 --> 00:27:57.660 position:63%
or to be prescriptive to a
creator of what a show needs

00:27:57.660 --> 00:27:59.820 position:63%
to be or a film needs to be.

00:27:59.820 --> 00:28:02.080 position:63%
But if you know exactly
what keeps people watching,

00:28:02.080 --> 00:28:04.440 position:63%
I mean, viewers are more
distracted than ever, how can

00:28:04.440 --> 00:28:06.660 position:63%
that not change what you make?

00:28:06.660 --> 00:28:09.820 position:63%
When we first started,
obviously the inclination was

00:28:09.820 --> 00:28:11.380 position:63%
let's try to measure everything.

00:28:11.380 --> 00:28:13.180 position:63%
One of my favorite
stories about this was at

00:28:13.180 --> 00:28:14.600 position:63%
the launch of House of Cards.

00:28:14.600 --> 00:28:17.840 position:63%
For the first week when
House of Cards came on,

00:28:17.840 --> 00:28:21.280 position:63%
at the one minute mark, a
lot of people turned it off.

00:28:21.280 --> 00:28:22.860 position:63%
And it turns out at that one minute mark,

00:28:22.860 --> 00:28:24.980 position:63%
Francis Underwood kills a dog.

00:28:24.980 --> 00:28:28.120 position:63%
Oh. And then another thing happened.

00:28:28.120 --> 00:28:29.660 position:63%
He looks up at the camera and starts doing

00:28:29.660 --> 00:28:30.740 position:63%
direct address to the camera.

00:28:31.860 --> 00:28:33.420 position:63%
I have no patience for useless things.

00:28:34.460 --> 00:28:36.020 position:63%
I think if you would've
looked at that data

00:28:36.020 --> 00:28:39.260 position:63%
and said, "Whatever happened
at minute one, change it."

00:28:39.260 --> 00:28:42.080 position:63%
And what's interesting is those
two things define the show.

00:28:43.220 --> 00:28:46.100 position:63%
While it is really important
to know what people like,

00:28:46.100 --> 00:28:48.940 position:63%
I think I always tell my
team, we're in the business

00:28:48.940 --> 00:28:50.300 position:63%
of creating demand

00:28:50.300 --> 00:28:53.900 position:63%
and the judgment of this each
individual creative executive

00:28:53.900 --> 00:28:56.080 position:63%
is very important.

00:28:56.080 --> 00:28:57.720 position:63%
When Squid Game first came out, there was

00:28:57.720 --> 00:28:59.680 position:63%
so much debate internally.

00:28:59.680 --> 00:29:03.140 position:63%
When it's a death game, people
only like watching death

00:29:03.140 --> 00:29:05.100 position:63%
game with young people.

00:29:05.100 --> 00:29:09.520 position:63%
They don't like looking at
older people, poor people

00:29:09.520 --> 00:29:12.500 position:63%
play these games, but we were wrong.

00:29:12.500 --> 00:29:14.400 position:63%
And I believe that audiences really like

00:29:14.400 --> 00:29:16.040 position:63%
to be surprised, right?

00:29:16.040 --> 00:29:17.800 position:63%
They want something that's familiar,

00:29:17.800 --> 00:29:20.560 position:63%
but in that familiarity,
they want to be surprised.

00:29:23.720 --> 00:29:26.320 position:63%
Turning movies and shows
into mere data might sound like

00:29:26.320 --> 00:29:28.720 position:63%
the opposite of creativity, but like it

00:29:28.720 --> 00:29:29.960 position:63%
or not, it's becoming a bigger

00:29:29.960 --> 00:29:31.900 position:63%
and bigger part of how
entertainment is made.

00:29:33.280 --> 00:29:34.600 position:63%
And with generative AI,

00:29:34.600 --> 00:29:36.320 position:63%
the creative process itself can be

00:29:36.320 --> 00:29:37.660 position:63%
reduced to ones and zeros.

00:29:39.360 --> 00:29:42.020 position:63%
Some hope that AI-powered
filmmaking tools can offer new

00:29:42.020 --> 00:29:44.640 position:63%
creative options and potential
cost savings at a tough

00:29:44.640 --> 00:29:48.440 position:63%
moment for the industry, but
AI also risks losing the human

00:29:48.440 --> 00:29:50.100 position:63%
touch in a way that many viewers

00:29:50.100 --> 00:29:52.060 position:63%
and creators find profoundly off-putting.

00:29:52.900 --> 00:29:54.780 position:63%
We have to be very cautious about AI.

00:29:54.780 --> 00:29:56.640 position:63%
It's made of stolen work.

00:29:56.640 --> 00:29:58.960 position:63%
It's a regurgitation of old work.

00:29:58.960 --> 00:30:00.020 position:63%
I've never seen a technology

00:30:00.020 --> 00:30:02.360 position:63%
advancing so rapidly,

00:30:02.360 --> 00:30:04.900 position:63%
so completely rejected by the public.

00:30:04.900 --> 00:30:07.180 position:63%
How can Hollywood thread this needle?

00:30:07.180 --> 00:30:09.400 position:63%
Ben Affleck and Netflix's chief product

00:30:09.400 --> 00:30:11.880 position:63%
and technology officer,
Elizabeth Stone, are hoping

00:30:11.880 --> 00:30:13.120 position:63%
that they've hit on a solution.

00:30:14.060 --> 00:30:17.220 position:63%
You said you were terrified of
AI when you saw it at first.

00:30:17.220 --> 00:30:18.220 position:63%
What scared you?

00:30:19.100 --> 00:30:21.840 position:63%
The first thought was like,
oh my God, we're finished.

00:30:21.840 --> 00:30:23.960 position:63%
You know, this, if you
can just type something in

00:30:23.960 --> 00:30:28.360 position:63%
and it appears and it seems
realistic, like what's the use

00:30:28.360 --> 00:30:30.780 position:63%
of actors and why do we
need to be making movies?

00:30:30.780 --> 00:30:33.080 position:63%
And that, you know, horrified me.

00:30:33.080 --> 00:30:35.280 position:63%
I quickly learned that wasn't possible.

00:30:35.280 --> 00:30:37.680 position:63%
It was very difficult. In fact,
it struggled doing a lot of

00:30:37.680 --> 00:30:40.100 position:63%
what you would consider very basic things.

00:30:40.100 --> 00:30:41.760 position:63%
If you type something into ChatGPT

00:30:41.760 --> 00:30:43.360 position:63%
and it gives you an
impressive answer, it sort

00:30:43.360 --> 00:30:45.680 position:63%
of seems like magic, but it's not.

00:30:45.680 --> 00:30:48.780 position:63%
It's numbers. It's a
probabilistic mechanism.

00:30:48.780 --> 00:30:50.760 position:63%
It's matrix multiplication, basically.

00:30:50.760 --> 00:30:53.840 position:63%
It's predicting what the next
token is or the next pixel is.

00:30:53.840 --> 00:30:57.200 position:63%
Everyone's a little scared.
Some people are a lot scared.

00:30:57.200 --> 00:30:58.961 position:63%
I don't think we should be scared.

00:30:58.961 --> 00:31:02.201 position:63%
If I look at the technology
over time in the entertainment

00:31:02.201 --> 00:31:05.941 position:63%
space, cameras, animation, visual effects,

00:31:05.941 --> 00:31:09.641 position:63%
they expanded creativity,
investment, opportunity.

00:31:09.641 --> 00:31:11.381 position:63%
So at least in the world of technology

00:31:11.381 --> 00:31:13.401 position:63%
and entertainment at Netflix,
I think there's way more

00:31:13.401 --> 00:31:15.901 position:63%
to be excited about
than to be scared about.

00:31:15.901 --> 00:31:18.261 position:63%
So this fear led you
to build an AI company.

00:31:18.261 --> 00:31:19.401 position:63%
What can it actually do?

00:31:20.421 --> 00:31:23.561 position:63%
Lots of things, but
what it has to do is work

00:31:23.561 --> 00:31:25.041 position:63%
with human filmmakers.

00:31:25.041 --> 00:31:27.821 position:63%
In other words, it's a
technology that is designed

00:31:27.821 --> 00:31:30.981 position:63%
to work hand in glove with
the way we work now, which is

00:31:30.981 --> 00:31:34.441 position:63%
to go out and film part of
your movie every single day.

00:31:34.441 --> 00:31:36.261 position:63%
And then the model trains from that

00:31:36.261 --> 00:31:39.601 position:63%
and becomes familiar with that filmmaker's

00:31:39.601 --> 00:31:41.421 position:63%
aesthetic, approach, work.

00:31:41.421 --> 00:31:42.701 position:63%
I didn't want to build something

00:31:42.701 --> 00:31:44.621 position:63%
that stole anything from anyone.

00:31:44.621 --> 00:31:45.881 position:63%
It didn't want to say, "Oh, well, did you

00:31:45.881 --> 00:31:49.321 position:63%
or did you not train on
some movie that is one

00:31:49.321 --> 00:31:52.021 position:63%
of my peers made?" So that we
could say honestly to people,

00:31:52.021 --> 00:31:54.101 position:63%
"No, this hasn't extracted
anything from you."

00:31:54.101 --> 00:31:56.182 position:63%
So can we get a little
bit more specific about then

00:31:56.182 --> 00:31:59.982 position:63%
what does this make better,
faster, maybe cheaper, next?

00:32:01.122 --> 00:32:04.602 position:63%
So the first kind of place
that distresses is things

00:32:04.602 --> 00:32:07.122 position:63%
that historically have been very expensive

00:32:07.122 --> 00:32:09.522 position:63%
and time-consuming to do in
the traditional visual effects

00:32:09.522 --> 00:32:12.742 position:63%
model, this can do a lot
more easily and more quickly.

00:32:12.742 --> 00:32:16.082 position:63%
And putting actors in another environment

00:32:16.082 --> 00:32:18.762 position:63%
or cleaning up shots where you don't want

00:32:18.762 --> 00:32:20.282 position:63%
to see the stuntman or the harness

00:32:20.282 --> 00:32:24.062 position:63%
or you want to change
aspects of the light that

00:32:24.062 --> 00:32:25.162 position:63%
you couldn't change before

00:32:25.162 --> 00:32:28.242 position:63%
because one change would
affect the overall image

00:32:28.242 --> 00:32:29.502 position:63%
or whatever it may be.

00:32:29.502 --> 00:32:32.002 position:63%
It's very useful in that sense.

00:32:32.002 --> 00:32:33.862 position:63%
It is not useful for

00:32:33.862 --> 00:32:35.502 position:63%
and was never designed to be like, oh,

00:32:35.502 --> 00:32:37.242 position:63%
just make your movie with Inner Positive.

00:32:37.242 --> 00:32:38.682 position:63%
That's not possible.
It's not going to happen.

00:32:41.462 --> 00:32:43.662 position:63%
Interpositive's technology
was used in the making

00:32:43.662 --> 00:32:46.422 position:63%
of Affleck's Netflix feature
Animals released in October.

00:32:47.302 --> 00:32:50.722 position:63%
But Netflix declined to show
us those AI tools in action

00:32:50.722 --> 00:32:53.063 position:63%
or point out examples in
the film where it was used.

00:32:54.223 --> 00:32:56.423 position:63%
It's like if I invite
you to a magic show

00:32:56.423 --> 00:32:58.143 position:63%
and the first thing you do
come in, when you come in

00:32:58.143 --> 00:33:00.523 position:63%
and sit down is I go, by the
way, there's two rabbits.

00:33:00.523 --> 00:33:02.903 position:63%
There's one under here.
This guy's a plant.

00:33:02.903 --> 00:33:05.503 position:63%
The wire's up there. So
now it ruined it, right?

00:33:05.503 --> 00:33:06.943 position:63%
So I go, oh, this is how it's used.

00:33:06.943 --> 00:33:09.943 position:63%
And people start to look for
or identify those things.

00:33:10.963 --> 00:33:12.123 position:63%
In addition to Animals,

00:33:12.123 --> 00:33:15.763 position:63%
Netflix says it's used AI in
the making of about 300 titles,

00:33:15.763 --> 00:33:18.343 position:63%
such as the docu-series
The American Experiment

00:33:18.343 --> 00:33:20.360 position:63%
and the sports drama series, Brazil 70.

00:33:21.263 --> 00:33:25.063 position:63%
Like recreating a stadium
during a football match,

00:33:25.063 --> 00:33:27.783 position:63%
which is something that you
could do, but not necessarily -

00:33:27.783 --> 00:33:28.803 position:63%
It's not as well.

00:33:28.803 --> 00:33:31.263 position:63%
It would've been janky
looking and kind of crummy

00:33:31.263 --> 00:33:33.883 position:63%
and this is just a better way of doing it.

00:33:33.883 --> 00:33:36.023 position:63%
In fact, none of this is entirely new.

00:33:37.503 --> 00:33:39.683 position:63%
Machine learning, the way I learned it

00:33:39.683 --> 00:33:40.923 position:63%
and why I understand this is

00:33:40.923 --> 00:33:42.483 position:63%
because like convolutional neural networks

00:33:42.483 --> 00:33:43.663 position:63%
to do feature detection

00:33:43.663 --> 00:33:46.203 position:63%
and edge extraction for
the conventional, you know,

00:33:46.203 --> 00:33:49.163 position:63%
visual effects workflow,
that technology is

00:33:49.163 --> 00:33:53.004 position:63%
how we've been working in
visual effects for some time.

00:33:53.004 --> 00:33:54.804 position:63%
Like I did this movie Air

00:33:54.804 --> 00:33:57.184 position:63%
and Matt's character had
a wedding ring on it.

00:33:57.184 --> 00:33:59.844 position:63%
And when we showed the test
audience, everybody's confused.

00:33:59.844 --> 00:34:01.584 position:63%
They were like, wait a
minute, he has a wedding ring

00:34:01.584 --> 00:34:02.704 position:63%
and where's his wife?

00:34:02.704 --> 00:34:05.604 position:63%
And the character he was
based on had this whole long

00:34:05.604 --> 00:34:07.884 position:63%
backstory that really had
nothing to do with this.

00:34:07.884 --> 00:34:09.424 position:63%
So I though, okay, well maybe it's easier

00:34:09.424 --> 00:34:10.504 position:63%
to just take the wedding ring off.

00:34:10.504 --> 00:34:12.764 position:63%
So he wrote like a Python
script that just went

00:34:12.764 --> 00:34:16.544 position:63%
and learned what the colors
were, how they were shaped

00:34:16.544 --> 00:34:18.164 position:63%
that made up the image of the wedding ring

00:34:18.164 --> 00:34:19.804 position:63%
and removed them and replaced them, right?

00:34:19.804 --> 00:34:22.624 position:63%
So it's been present for a
while in some ways, in a lot

00:34:22.624 --> 00:34:24.944 position:63%
of ways without people knowing.

00:34:24.944 --> 00:34:28.664 position:63%
Every technology, I
would say, gives something

00:34:28.664 --> 00:34:30.604 position:63%
to filmmakers and takes something away

00:34:30.604 --> 00:34:32.564 position:63%
or at least changes
something fundamentally.

00:34:32.564 --> 00:34:34.364 position:63%
Is AI actually just a tool

00:34:34.364 --> 00:34:35.764 position:63%
or something fundamentally different?

00:34:37.064 --> 00:34:39.624 position:63%
To be a technologist, you have

00:34:39.624 --> 00:34:41.984 position:63%
to believe it makes fundamental change.

00:34:42.984 --> 00:34:45.484 position:63%
But I do think AI will be one

00:34:45.484 --> 00:34:48.405 position:63%
of many tools in the filmmaker toolkit.

00:34:48.405 --> 00:34:49.865 position:63%
I hope it's revolutionary

00:34:49.865 --> 00:34:51.665 position:63%
for the filmmakers who want to use it.

00:34:51.665 --> 00:34:55.485 position:63%
I think it will be, but I
think it probably oversells it

00:34:55.485 --> 00:34:58.365 position:63%
when you say it's revolutionary
or it's fundamental.

00:34:58.365 --> 00:35:00.825 position:63%
I think people assume that
means it took a human out

00:35:00.825 --> 00:35:01.905 position:63%
and that's not what I mean by it.

00:35:02.885 --> 00:35:04.585 position:63%
Hollywood is totally
freaked out about AI.

00:35:05.425 --> 00:35:07.525 position:63%
Where are they right and
where are they wrong?

00:35:07.525 --> 00:35:08.585 position:63%
Hollywood has a rich history

00:35:08.585 --> 00:35:10.965 position:63%
of being totally freaked out
about the next everything,

00:35:10.965 --> 00:35:12.665 position:63%
including back then, remember Netflix too.

00:35:13.705 --> 00:35:16.345 position:63%
And I think that that was
true of pay television.

00:35:16.345 --> 00:35:18.485 position:63%
It was true of home video.

00:35:18.485 --> 00:35:21.165 position:63%
So I don't think this is any different.

00:35:21.165 --> 00:35:23.205 position:63%
I do think that people will figure out

00:35:23.205 --> 00:35:25.085 position:63%
where this actually serves the business

00:35:26.165 --> 00:35:27.805 position:63%
and embrace that versus being

00:35:27.805 --> 00:35:29.485 position:63%
afraid of how it's going to replace you.

00:35:29.485 --> 00:35:30.645 position:63%
It's not going to replace you. Someone

00:35:30.645 --> 00:35:32.685 position:63%
who uses it better than you might.

00:35:32.685 --> 00:35:34.865 position:63%
Has no one lost their job because of AI?

00:35:34.865 --> 00:35:37.025 position:63%
I mean, you run tech
and product at Netflix.

00:35:37.025 --> 00:35:39.625 position:63%
People say like, how could
this not mean less fewer people

00:35:39.625 --> 00:35:42.240 position:63%
doing this one thing if you can?

00:35:42.240 --> 00:35:44.465 position:63%
Well, it depends how
big your aspirations are.

00:35:44.465 --> 00:35:47.486 position:63%
You would rather have more
titles that are all amazing

00:35:47.486 --> 00:35:49.466 position:63%
using the budget we have
today than figure out how

00:35:49.466 --> 00:35:50.666 position:63%
to have a smaller budget.

00:35:50.666 --> 00:35:53.606 position:63%
So I think that it's not as
inconsistent as you think

00:35:53.606 --> 00:35:57.086 position:63%
to say maybe to do this one
task, I need fewer people,

00:35:57.086 --> 00:35:58.766 position:63%
but I'm going to do more tasks so

00:35:58.766 --> 00:36:00.306 position:63%
that I can have better impact.

00:36:00.306 --> 00:36:03.306 position:63%
And what I think is interesting
is this interview even

00:36:03.306 --> 00:36:06.446 position:63%
sort of, I think validates
this thesis about

00:36:06.446 --> 00:36:08.426 position:63%
how drawn we are to fearful things.

00:36:08.426 --> 00:36:10.486 position:63%
But aren't you scared? Aren't
you scared? Who's not afraid?

00:36:10.486 --> 00:36:12.526 position:63%
Aren't we afraid? Can we
get some afraid clips?

00:36:12.526 --> 00:36:15.426 position:63%
Who wants to... And it's
like, no, I'm not that afraid.

00:36:15.426 --> 00:36:17.326 position:63%
I don't think it's going
to destroy creativity.

00:36:27.506 --> 00:36:29.446 position:63%
When we got to Korea
for our final interview

00:36:29.446 --> 00:36:31.466 position:63%
with Sarandos, the news about Netflix had

00:36:31.466 --> 00:36:32.626 position:63%
taken a dramatic turn.

00:36:33.466 --> 00:36:35.846 position:63%
For months, observers had
wondered if the company was

00:36:35.846 --> 00:36:37.086 position:63%
planning big changes.

00:36:39.806 --> 00:36:42.507 position:63%
And in October, those
changes arrived with a bang.

00:36:43.407 --> 00:36:46.027 position:63%
Developing news coming
out of Netflix headquarters.

00:36:46.027 --> 00:36:48.367 position:63%
The streaming giant is cutting 6%

00:36:48.367 --> 00:36:50.887 position:63%
of its workforce about a thousand jobs.

00:36:52.267 --> 00:36:55.067 position:63%
The layoffs were the biggest
in the company's history

00:36:55.067 --> 00:36:56.487 position:63%
and were accompanied by the firing

00:36:56.487 --> 00:36:58.827 position:63%
of two high ranking executives,

00:36:58.827 --> 00:37:00.527 position:63%
head of film, Dan Lin,

00:37:00.527 --> 00:37:02.807 position:63%
and chief content officer Bela Bajaria,

00:37:02.807 --> 00:37:05.527 position:63%
who led Netflix's global
production strategy for years.

00:37:06.447 --> 00:37:08.747 position:63%
Okay.
Since we last talked,

00:37:09.767 --> 00:37:11.947 position:63%
dramatic shakeup at the top of Netflix.

00:37:11.947 --> 00:37:15.907 position:63%
What wasn't working and
why make these changes now?

00:37:15.907 --> 00:37:19.087 position:63%
So we have a very large employee base

00:37:19.087 --> 00:37:21.547 position:63%
and we've been growing
and growing and growing.

00:37:21.547 --> 00:37:24.007 position:63%
And many companies
routinely will go through

00:37:24.007 --> 00:37:26.367 position:63%
and do these kind of adjustments.

00:37:26.367 --> 00:37:29.307 position:63%
We haven't done something
like this in about five years.

00:37:29.307 --> 00:37:33.787 position:63%
So it was a readjustment of a
lot of groups within Netflix

00:37:33.787 --> 00:37:35.507 position:63%
who do what we do every day.

00:37:35.507 --> 00:37:37.247 position:63%
And I should mention, these are great

00:37:37.247 --> 00:37:39.007 position:63%
people who did great work.

00:37:39.007 --> 00:37:41.367 position:63%
And fortunately, it's
a very rare occurrence.

00:37:41.367 --> 00:37:43.588 position:63%
Obviously my heart goes out
to the folks who were impacted

00:37:43.588 --> 00:37:45.848 position:63%
by it, but I think it has made the

00:37:45.848 --> 00:37:48.208 position:63%
company healthier for the future.

00:37:48.208 --> 00:37:51.228 position:63%
How about Bela and Dan leaving?

00:37:51.228 --> 00:37:53.228 position:63%
Bela and Dan both did
a fantastic job for us

00:37:53.228 --> 00:37:56.028 position:63%
and I'm very proud of what they've done.

00:37:56.028 --> 00:37:59.368 position:63%
Every once in a while when
you're in my seat, you have

00:37:59.368 --> 00:38:02.128 position:63%
to look forward and say
there's people who are good

00:38:02.128 --> 00:38:03.968 position:63%
for the business at one stage who may

00:38:03.968 --> 00:38:05.948 position:63%
or may not be good for
it at the next stage.

00:38:05.948 --> 00:38:08.368 position:63%
And you want to get ahead
of that as far as you can.

00:38:08.368 --> 00:38:10.568 position:63%
I think as Netflix has grown and matured

00:38:10.568 --> 00:38:12.768 position:63%
and the business has grown
and matured around us,

00:38:12.768 --> 00:38:14.128 position:63%
that hit rate really matters.

00:38:14.128 --> 00:38:17.548 position:63%
And you've got to get ahead of,
are we on a path to continue

00:38:17.548 --> 00:38:19.928 position:63%
to maintain that hit rate

00:38:19.928 --> 00:38:22.548 position:63%
in the US particularly, but
everywhere in the world?

00:38:22.548 --> 00:38:24.508 position:63%
And I'd say the business is
working everywhere in the world,

00:38:24.508 --> 00:38:27.428 position:63%
including the US, but we should be doing

00:38:27.428 --> 00:38:29.048 position:63%
better in the US than we were.

00:38:29.048 --> 00:38:32.388 position:63%
And we've got to really focus
on our ability to do that.

00:38:32.388 --> 00:38:34.068 position:63%
I'm sure these weren't
hard decisions for you,

00:38:34.068 --> 00:38:35.548 position:63%
but you've been the architect

00:38:35.548 --> 00:38:39.109 position:63%
of Netflix's content for years.
How much of this is on you?

00:38:39.109 --> 00:38:42.929 position:63%
Well, ultimately everything's
on me and Greg, co-CEOs.

00:38:42.929 --> 00:38:45.669 position:63%
But I would say that when you
say I'm the architect of it,

00:38:45.669 --> 00:38:48.909 position:63%
you have to continue to
evolve the architecture.

00:38:48.909 --> 00:38:50.529 position:63%
I personally think that

00:38:50.529 --> 00:38:53.429 position:63%
what distinguishes Netflix
from everywhere else, including

00:38:54.569 --> 00:38:56.109 position:63%
free ad supported services

00:38:56.109 --> 00:39:00.209 position:63%
and social media is the
ambition of the storytelling.

00:39:00.209 --> 00:39:02.589 position:63%
I don't believe that we
compete better for that

00:39:02.589 --> 00:39:05.249 position:63%
by chasing everything
that people just happen

00:39:05.249 --> 00:39:07.689 position:63%
to spend five seconds on television on.

00:39:07.689 --> 00:39:09.169 position:63%
And I think when people
see things like, "Well,

00:39:09.169 --> 00:39:11.869 position:63%
what about AI?" I think
what's going to happen is

00:39:11.869 --> 00:39:16.149 position:63%
that people will desire more ambitious,

00:39:16.149 --> 00:39:18.249 position:63%
more accomplished storytelling in a world

00:39:18.249 --> 00:39:20.569 position:63%
that is flooded with slop.

00:39:20.569 --> 00:39:21.729 position:63%
There's more to watch than ever,

00:39:21.729 --> 00:39:23.969 position:63%
and yet people still complain
there's nothing to watch.

00:39:23.969 --> 00:39:26.889 position:63%
Is that the fundamental problem
that Netflix needs to solve?

00:39:27.989 --> 00:39:29.209 position:63%
Well, I don't think so. I do think

00:39:29.209 --> 00:39:33.349 position:63%
that we can make mistakes
about how we present new things

00:39:33.349 --> 00:39:35.630 position:63%
and bury things that you might be looking

00:39:35.630 --> 00:39:38.270 position:63%
for under new things that
you're not coming for.

00:39:38.270 --> 00:39:39.510 position:63%
And that's

00:39:39.510 --> 00:39:41.630 position:63%
what I'm really excited
actually about AI innovation

00:39:41.630 --> 00:39:45.390 position:63%
for our business is what it
can bring to post-production,

00:39:45.390 --> 00:39:47.370 position:63%
pre-production and discovery.

00:39:47.370 --> 00:39:50.430 position:63%
Can it do better and better
at figuring out mood states

00:39:50.430 --> 00:39:52.770 position:63%
and based on what you're
looking at right now,

00:39:52.770 --> 00:39:54.610 position:63%
are you interested in this versus that

00:39:54.610 --> 00:39:56.110 position:63%
and change in real time?

00:39:56.110 --> 00:40:00.130 position:63%
Those things are all
incredibly more attainable

00:40:00.130 --> 00:40:01.670 position:63%
with these new AI powered tools.

00:40:01.670 --> 00:40:03.770 position:63%
When someone says there's
nothing to watch, it's just

00:40:03.770 --> 00:40:05.430 position:63%
that we're not matching your mood state.

00:40:05.430 --> 00:40:07.210 position:63%
It's in there. I promise
you it's in there,

00:40:08.090 --> 00:40:09.050 position:63%
but we're just not matching

00:40:09.050 --> 00:40:10.730 position:63%
your mood state in the discovery.

00:40:10.730 --> 00:40:12.070 position:63%
Does running Netflix ever feel a

00:40:12.070 --> 00:40:13.210 position:63%
little bit like Squid Game?

00:40:13.210 --> 00:40:15.170 position:63%
Like you won the game and now
there's just another game?

00:40:15.170 --> 00:40:16.510 position:63%
There's another game. Well,

00:40:16.510 --> 00:40:19.130 position:63%
I'm a believer in the infinite,
the theory of infinite,

00:40:19.130 --> 00:40:23.850 position:63%
the infinite game, that no one
has won the streaming wars.

00:40:23.850 --> 00:40:25.870 position:63%
The streaming wars are
continuing as we go.

00:40:25.870 --> 00:40:28.870 position:63%
Half of television engagement
in the United States is

00:40:28.870 --> 00:40:30.670 position:63%
still linear old television.

00:40:30.670 --> 00:40:32.810 position:63%
The game isn't over until you fold.

00:40:40.811 --> 00:40:43.151 position:63%
In showbiz, the only constant is change.

00:40:44.431 --> 00:40:46.971 position:63%
Tastes have always been
shifting and new players

00:40:46.971 --> 00:40:49.131 position:63%
and technologies are
always coming on the scene.

00:40:50.091 --> 00:40:53.071 position:63%
Today, the ground feels
more unstable than ever.

00:40:53.071 --> 00:40:54.511 position:63%
The rapid growth of YouTube share

00:40:54.511 --> 00:40:56.311 position:63%
of streaming is just one indication

00:40:56.311 --> 00:40:58.911 position:63%
of the challenges facing
paid streaming platforms.

00:41:00.191 --> 00:41:01.731 position:63%
Netflix, meanwhile, has evolved from

00:41:01.731 --> 00:41:03.371 position:63%
disruptor to mature business.

00:41:04.311 --> 00:41:07.451 position:63%
It's no longer in the heady
days of explosive growth,

00:41:07.451 --> 00:41:10.171 position:63%
but in today's media biz,
that's hard to come by.

00:41:11.280 --> 00:41:13.351 position:63%
10 years ago, you made this bet

00:41:13.351 --> 00:41:15.491 position:63%
that local stories would be global hits.

00:41:15.491 --> 00:41:17.651 position:63%
What's the next bet that's that big?

00:41:17.651 --> 00:41:18.711 position:63%
- There's two things that are

00:41:18.711 --> 00:41:20.271 position:63%
happening to the
entertainment world right now.

00:41:20.271 --> 00:41:22.531 position:63%
Yeah.
One is, is obviously AI

00:41:22.531 --> 00:41:25.531 position:63%
and how that's going to impact
production and storytelling.

00:41:25.531 --> 00:41:29.511 position:63%
And the other is the, the
audience requiring a certain level

00:41:29.511 --> 00:41:31.652 position:63%
of agency that they didn't used to.

00:41:31.652 --> 00:41:33.472 position:63%
So by way of example, the TV ratings

00:41:33.472 --> 00:41:35.932 position:63%
for professional sports now are very big,

00:41:35.932 --> 00:41:37.552 position:63%
and I think it's the interactivity of it.

00:41:37.552 --> 00:41:40.612 position:63%
It's the online betting
and prediction markets

00:41:40.612 --> 00:41:42.692 position:63%
and all those things are
actually pulling in the audience

00:41:42.692 --> 00:41:44.132 position:63%
and giving them more engagement.

00:41:44.132 --> 00:41:47.492 position:63%
I think the next wave of
entertainment technology should be

00:41:47.492 --> 00:41:48.852 position:63%
able to address both of those things.

00:41:52.092 --> 00:41:55.112 position:63%
So what is a movie in 20 years?

00:41:55.112 --> 00:41:57.992 position:63%
Like does it look like what
we think a movie is today?

00:41:58.932 --> 00:42:03.312 position:63%
I think there are going
to be types of content

00:42:03.312 --> 00:42:05.712 position:63%
that put you more in the story

00:42:05.712 --> 00:42:07.872 position:63%
or more immersive, more interactive.

00:42:07.872 --> 00:42:09.792 position:63%
That's going to be one flavor of content.

00:42:09.792 --> 00:42:10.812 position:63%
Then I think there's going to be

00:42:10.812 --> 00:42:12.012 position:63%
movies that are more traditional.

00:42:12.012 --> 00:42:13.352 position:63%
Then I think there's going to be things

00:42:13.352 --> 00:42:15.232 position:63%
that are completely GenAI created

00:42:15.232 --> 00:42:17.232 position:63%
and some people will enjoy that.

00:42:17.232 --> 00:42:19.892 position:63%
I think the future's going
to be not just one form

00:42:19.892 --> 00:42:21.112 position:63%
of entertainment.

00:42:21.112 --> 00:42:22.672 position:63%
I think it looks like what
a movie looks like today.

00:42:22.672 --> 00:42:25.292 position:63%
Yeah.
I mean, it's the same story that

00:42:25.292 --> 00:42:27.553 position:63%
Shakespeare wrote in 1610.

00:42:27.553 --> 00:42:32.013 position:63%
We are a species of animal that identifies

00:42:32.013 --> 00:42:33.333 position:63%
and understands ourselves

00:42:33.333 --> 00:42:35.773 position:63%
and our relationship to one
another through the sort

00:42:35.773 --> 00:42:38.493 position:63%
of actual stories that
we create around them.

00:42:38.493 --> 00:42:41.393 position:63%
I don't think that we're
going to fundamentally

00:42:41.393 --> 00:42:44.273 position:63%
change the way we tell
stories or the visual stories.

00:42:44.273 --> 00:42:46.473 position:63%
And we do need, you
know, humans in the loop

00:42:46.473 --> 00:42:50.033 position:63%
and actually human judgment
and expertise and taste.

00:42:50.033 --> 00:42:51.493 position:63%
And the human brain has

00:42:51.493 --> 00:42:54.353 position:63%
so many more neurons
than a hundred times the

00:42:54.353 --> 00:42:56.173 position:63%
biggest frontier model.

00:42:56.173 --> 00:42:58.333 position:63%
And there's some magic in
that that allows people

00:42:58.333 --> 00:43:01.333 position:63%
to get together and come up
with ideas and build new things.

00:43:42.854 --> 00:43:44.094 position:63%
Let's go play some games.

00:43:47.294 --> 00:43:50.154 position:63%
Can I be CEO now? Okay.

